Z||000007||Bob||10-05-2001||01:25 AM||||With a lot of power coming from a supercharged 3800 series II, which trans would hold up better? So, if I wanted to really trick out the suspension, an 87 would be better?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||10-05-2001||08:29 AM||||If you want to get into modifications an 85-87 is a better staring point.  Most components can be upgraded a lot easier to better brakes and handling.  A stock 88 GT was the best handling Fiero Pontiac built, but with the limited production run replacig suspension components and brake can be expensive.<P>It all will depend on your driving desires (habits)  I have both an 87 and 88.  My 87 , I bought new and still have the original suspension.  The car drives great and meets my needs for thrills with the limited use I make of it "pushing it" to my limits of my driving skills.<P>My 88 has had all the suspension components replaced (previous owner used the car in SCCAA events)with GM shocks.  Steering racks are expensive to replace,  Rebuilt ones are the only ones around and some rebuilt ones are of questionable quality.  It has a 3.4L with a t440 automatic now and is a lot faster than my driving skills permit with an unknown top end. I don't use the car for drag racing or road racing and it also meets my expectations for a fun to drive car.<P>You can expect a professional rebuild to cost between $4-10K, done by people who have done this sort of thing before.  Cost will be less the more you do yourself and your ability and your frustration limit.<P>The Fieros are fun cars to drive.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||10-05-2001||08:36 AM||||88 is the prefered, 84 is least desirable,85-87 is the most common.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jefrysuko||10-05-2001||10:05 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bob:<BR><B>With a lot of power coming from a supercharged 3800 series II, which trans would hold up better? So, if I wanted to really trick out the suspension, an 87 would be better?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes you can find drop Spindles, springs, brake kits, tubular A-arms and stuff like that much easier and CHEAPER for the 84-87.<P>I personally have an 88 GT that I am leaving stock and a 86 SE that I drive daily and plan to modify as time goes on.  That way I have the best of both worlds and I don't go broke trying to make modifications to the 88 suspension.<P>One other thing that might interest you is to run the RPO/Option codes on a car before you buy it.  I did that just recently and found out that Fieros came with alot of different spring rates.  My 88 has medium stiffness springs and makes for a good touring car.  While my 86 has the stiffest springs and is going to make one heck of an autocrosser.  At least that is my opinion.||||reg||1
