Z||000017||Bazooka||10-06-2001||09:43 PM||||Bob,   Check out 'Fiero Secrets' for Info/Modifications. Its at    <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html</A> <BR>************** <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>*********************************<BR>Originally posted by Bob:<BR>[B]Hey I am new to the whole Fiero scene, but I am really interested in building up a Fiero of my own. I just have a few starter questions, What are some sites that have Fiero info/modifications? How much time/money (rough estimate) goes into an engine swap (3800 Series II)? What year Fiero would be best to purchase, anything I should look for when buying a Fiero? Any tips to help me get on my way are gladly accepted. <BR>***************************************<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Leper||10-06-2001||09:44 PM||||There isn't anything inherently wrong with the 84's. The main things are that it only comes with the 2.5. The wiring is different so you can't swap in a 2.8 without swapping the complete wiring harness. (since you have to modify the wiring harness for most other, non 2.8L, engine swaps, it isn't as big of a problem.) The later engine cradles are designed with different mounting points that reduce drop throttle oversteer and (to a lesser extent) bumpsteer. In other words, if you are going to majorly modify a Fiero, an 84 is just as good as any other year.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Bob||10-07-2001||01:07 AM||||TTT||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||10-07-2001||01:41 AM||||Bob,<P>Nope, if the combo was not for sale in CA you can't do it. Well, you can but unless the smog tech is a complete moron they will send you packing off to BAR for recertification. <P>When you get to BAR, they will look up the engine in the Mitchell's book, notice there is no manual trans available in CA and wave bye-bye.<P>Now if you swap the engine and trans together, recertification is rather easy. It takes a few hours of you waiting while they check that you installed all of the equipment listed in the Mitchell's manual and using the VIN from the donor car you supplied. You must always make sure you supply a CA car's VIN or they will throw you out. They will type in the VIN, get the option list and they better see option YF5 (CA emissions).<P>So, use a CA donor car. Get the VIN, install the engine, get it recertified. It's remotely possible you could then put the Getrag in and find a shop that will ignore the manual trans during the bi-yearly tests, but that's a crap-shot.<P>||||reg||1
